  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nA deepening High Plains lee trough will lead to breezy to\noccasionally gusty SW winds for the rest of the afternoon and\ninto the early evening hours. The winds will eventually become\nlight southerly through the overnight. These light southerly\nwinds will lead to above normal lows tonight, with values only\nbottoming out in the upper 50s to lower 60s. We will be solidly\nin the warm sector throughout the weekend period. Highs will\ncontinue to remain above normal with highs in the upper 80s to\nlower 90s Saturday and Sunday. Lows will continue to remain mild\nwith values in the 50s and 60s. No major synoptic systems are \nexpected through the weekend, so the net result of a dry \nforecast is expected for the next several days.\n\nMore downsloping southwesterly flow aloft is expected Monday.\nLow level downsloping winds are expected as well. The net result\nis a very warm day Monday with highs in the lower 90s. Mild lows\nare also expected with values only bottoming out in the upper\n60s to lower 70s. Even with the hot temperatures and breezy\nwinds, significant fire weather concerns are not expected Monday\nas afternoon relative humidity levels will remain on the\nmoderate side. \n\nOn Tuesday, a deepening upper level low is expected to move\nacross Utah during the day. Low level moisture will continue to\nadvect northward across southwest Kansas. The combination of\nstronger winds aloft and increasing boundary layer moisture does\nspell out the concern for possible severe storms during the\nafternoon and evening hours Tuesday. Afternoon CAPE values may\nincrease in the 1000 J/kg to 1500 J/kg range, if the front makes\nit north across Kansas. Bulk shear may be strong enough for\nsupercells as well. We will have to watch where the front will\nend up, because if the front does surge to the south during the\nday, the brunt of the warm sector and associated instability may\nend up across the southern zones and even farther south into\nOklahoma and Texas. This is something to watch.\n\nAs far as remnant tropical moisture midweek, there may be a\nstarting trend in the ensembles to keep much of the moisture\neast and southeast of the forecast area. There has been a trend\nin the ensembles in the last 24 hours to either keep the highest\npops across the eastern zones or even possibly out of the FA. \nThis is a second thing to watch and to look if this drier trend \nin the ensembles continues to hold true.",
